From 465eed5b40a18d41ff12a242e862023c636bc6ee Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dom Cobley <popcornmix@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 12 Aug 2026 21:38:24 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 07/22] Resolution: select a native 3D display mode for
 stereoscopic playback

CResolutionUtils ignored the is3D argument - it was only logged - and scored
candidates by size, so a hardware 3D mode could never be chosen automatically:
a frame-packing mode reports its packed scanout (1920x2205) and always lost to
the plain 1080p mode. Two more gaps hid behind that one. No 3D mode ever made
it into the candidate list: the default whitelist demands candidates no smaller
than the mode in use, which a 4K desktop fails every 1080- or 720-line-based
HDMI 3D mode against, and a user whitelist excludes them by construction,
because the modes are offered as separate entries and nobody ticks
1920x2205p tab 23.98Hz. And the search demanded the layout the source happened
to use - a left_right stream autodetects to SPLIT_VERTICAL and so insisted on a
side-by-side mode - though HDMI 1.4 mandates frame packing and half
top-and-bottom at 24p and mandates side-by-side only for 1080i, so on a common
AV receiver EDID a 3D Blu-ray rip found nothing. Each way, the sink was never
switched into 3D and playback came out as two half-width images on a display
that was never told to expect them.

Match modes against one eye. A display mode has to fit one eye rather than the
packed frame, and the searches do match on size: a full side-by-side 3840x1080
frame otherwise matches a 3840x2160 mode, because a mode whose width matches is
accepted while its height is merely not exceeded ('width == iScreenWidth &&
height <= iScreenHeight + 32'), and no 1920x1080 mode matches either clause to
compete with it. A full packing is wider (side-by-side) or taller
(top-and-bottom) than any single frame plausibly is, so halve that axis; a half
packing already describes one eye and is left alone, as is all 2D content.

Let a whitelisted timing license the sink's 3D variants of it. A 3D variant is
not a display mode anyone chooses in its own right: it is the same video timing
as the 2D mode - 1920x1080p23.98, its top-and-bottom variant and its
frame-packed variant are all 74.176 MHz with identical timings, the kernel
doubling the CRTC timing for frame packing - transported differently, and which
transport is wanted follows from the stereo arrangement rather than from taste.
Whitelisting 1920x1080p23.98 is then enough to reach frame packing, and an
existing whitelist gains 3D without being touched, its entries already being
the 2D timings the rule keys on; a 3D mode whitelisted outright licenses
itself, a mode being the same timing as itself. Deriving that timing is not
uniform: a half mode transmits one eye inside an ordinary frame and already
reports the 2D geometry; frame packing reports its packed scanout, so divide
the gap and the second eye back out - iBlanking is set for a frame-packing mode
and nothing else, which keeps the two cases apart. With the default whitelist,
measure its "don't downgrade below the current mode" floor against the size of
one eye rather than the mode in use: not downgrading is a sharpness heuristic,
and it has nothing to say when the alternative is not being stereoscopic at
all. Scan type still has to match, and the half refresh rates the default
whitelist leaves out are no more playable in 3D than in 2D. The 3D modes are
dropped from the whitelist setting's options, since they are no longer a
choice: their geometry reads as a resolution the display does not have, and
picking one adds nothing that whitelisting its 2D timing has not already done.
videoscreen.screenmode keeps them, which is how a 3D mode is selected by hand.

Output the split arrangement the display can receive. The layout a display mode
has to carry follows from the GUI stereo mode: SPLIT_VERTICAL is side-by-side,
SPLIT_HORIZONTAL is top-and-bottom (which covers frame packing), and every
other mode puts a single image on the whole screen and so needs a 2D mode. That
is independent of the source layout, because the renderer crops the correct eye
whichever way the screen is split - the source says nothing about how to split
it; the display does, because the arrangement is what the sink is signalled. So
before the search runs, ask what the display can present and prefer the better
of the two arrangements: the one whose mode matches at an earlier refresh tier,
then the larger mode at that tier, which is frame packing carrying a whole
frame per eye where a half mode carries half of one. Neither arrangement having
a mode changes nothing, which is what keeps a display with no native 3D modes
working as it does today - that is a display the viewer switches into 3D by
hand, and how the frame is split is then theirs to decide. Nor is an
arrangement adopted that the renderer cannot produce, since SetStereoMode()
applies nothing at all for an unsupported one and the viewer would end up with
no 3D rather than the arrangement they asked for. The choice is made in
SetStereoMode(), which resolves what to apply and owns the setting write, so
AUTO, the preferred-mode setting and an explicit choice from the 3D dialog all
pass through it and the arrangement is settled before the search runs. The
stream parameters the modes are matched against come from the player, which is
also why nothing changes while nothing is playing. The per-eye derivation is
shared with the search for the same reason: the display mode a 3840x1080
side-by-side frame needs is found by its 1920x1080 eye, and the arrangement has
to be chosen against that same size or it can prefer a layout the search then
rejects. The choice is not reflected back into the "Stereoscopic 3D mode"
setting spinner if 3D is turned on there directly during playback, that path
being the write itself rather than a request to apply a mode; every other route
goes through SetStereoMode().

For a stereoscopic arrangement, then, pick the largest whitelisted mode that
natively carries the matching 3D flag. Largest, so frame packing wins over a
half-resolution mode. Try the same refresh tiers as the standard search -
exact, then double, then 3:2 pulldown, gated on the same settings - because 3D
EDIDs commonly omit rates the 2D modes have (e.g. no 30Hz 3D mode). Finally try
the desktop refresh rate: content whose rate has no 3D mode at all (e.g. ~48fps
where the 3D modes are 24/50/60Hz) would otherwise fall through to the 2D
desktop resolution and drop out of 3D entirely; this keeps it stereoscopic at
the rate the GUI was using. Within that pass the mode is chosen by refresh rate
and layout, never by size. For 2D output, erase the natively-3D modes from the
candidate list instead and let the existing tiers run. "Watch as 2D" (MONO)
needs 2D output despite is3D being true - the source is still stereoscopic, and
the per-eye derivation depends on that, but only one eye is shown, full screen,
and a half 3D mode's fPixelRatio is double, which GetResInfo() only compensates
for while the GUI is in the matching split mode, so the eye came out squeezed
into half the width. The search takes the requested stereo mode rather than the
current one: SetStereoMode() only records it and Flip() makes it current a
frame later, so a search triggered by the change itself - which is exactly when
the display mode has to be re-chosen - would otherwise see the mode being left
behind and pick for that instead.

The D3DPRESENTFLAG_MODEMASK comparisons the tiers use cannot tell a 3D mode
from a 2D one by themselves, because GetResInfo() OR-s the stereo mode still in
effect onto every mode it returns. Which side of a comparison carries a 3D bit
natively, and whether the other has had one OR-ed in, therefore depends on
whether the deferred mode change has flipped yet. So mask those bits out of
both sides whenever 2D output is wanted, which makes the outcome independent of
that timing, and mask them out unconditionally while building the default
whitelist: there a mode is a candidate on its size and scan type, comparing 3D
bits only ever ties by accident, and a mode wrongly excluded is invisible to
every search below. A natively-3D mode is no use as that list's "don't
downgrade" floor either, once 2D output is wanted: a frame-packing mode reports
its packed scanout, which no 2D mode can reach, so the list came out empty.
Measure against the desktop mode, the 2D baseline, instead.

Finally, re-run the search when the stereo mode changes during playback.
ApplyStereoMode() only re-chose a resolution while nothing was playing, so
switching to "play as 2D" and back left the sink in the mode playback had
started in. The player owns the stream parameters the search needs, so ask it
to re-run the search rather than calling it directly.

Other windowing backends are unaffected: none of them flags a mode MODE3DSBS or
MODE3DTB, and Windows stereo uses RenderStereoMode::HARDWAREBASED, so neither
branch fires there.

Don't gate the 3D refresh tiers on the whitelist settings

The double refresh rate and 3:2 pulldown tiers were gated on
videoscreen.whitelistdoublerefreshrate and videoscreen.whitelistpulldown, the
settings the 2D searches use, on the grounds that a search should be as
conservative about changing the refresh rate for 3D as it is for 2D. That is the
wrong comparison. The 2D searches turn a multiple down in favour of the content
rate on the same display mode; for 3D the alternative is usually not the content
rate at all, because a 3D EDID carries far sparser rate coverage - a sink offering
side-by-side at 50, 59.94 and 60Hz and nothing below them is ordinary - so it is
no 3D mode, and 2D playback of 3D content.

Which is what it did: 30fps side-by-side content on such a sink, with a user
whitelist and both settings off, skipped the 60Hz side-by-side mode and took the
desktop rate tier's 50Hz one, juddering 5:3, from a display that had exactly the
mode wanted. The desktop rate tier is not gated on those settings either, so
gating the multiples only ever turned down the better of the two.

So try the content rate, double it, a 3:2 pulldown rate and the desktop rate,
always, and let the tier order be what expresses the preference between them.

That fixes the tiers as a list of four, so name them by index in the log. Which
tier a mode came from is the difference between a considered choice and a last
resort, and nothing about the mode settled on says which it was: "Matched a
native 3D mode 1920x1080 @ 50.000000 Hz (31)" read like an exact match for 50fps
content rather than a 30fps compromise, and reading it that way cost a round trip
through a log.
